---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How we verify"
  description="Our editorial method: every page cites primary official sources, carries a verification date, and is flagged for review before it goes stale."
  alternates={{ en: '/how-we-verify', it: '/it/how-we-verify' }}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How we verify</h1>
    <p>
      This site's value is accuracy. Relocation rules change, and stale information
      is worse than none — so we make verification a mechanical part of publishing,
      not an afterthought.
    </p>

    <h2>Every page is sourced</h2>
    <p>
      A page cannot be published without at least one <strong>primary source</strong>
      — an official Italian authority (Agenzia delle Entrate, Ministero dell'Interno,
      Ministero della Salute, the relevant comune or health authority) or the EU's
      Your Europe portal. This is enforced by the build itself: the content schema
      rejects any page missing its sources.
    </p>

    <h2>Every page is dated</h2>
    <p>
      Each page shows when it was <strong>last verified</strong> and when it is
      <strong>next due for review</strong>. An automated check flags overdue pages so
      they get re-checked rather than silently trusted. When a page is past its review
      date you'll see a "review due" badge instead of a verification date.
    </p>

    <h2>We work at the right level of detail</h2>
    <p>
      Italy's rules apply at three levels, and we mirror that exactly:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>National</strong> — the codice fiscale is uniform across Italy.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Comune</strong> — residency registration (anagrafe) is run by each
        comune, so the office, booking system and forms are local.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Regional</strong> — the health service (SSN) authority depends on
        your region.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Where we don't yet have a verified local detail, we say so plainly and point
      you to the official office — we never invent specifics.
    </p>

    <h2>Drafts are labelled</h2>
    <p>
      Pages still awaiting a full primary-source check carry a visible "draft" note.
      They are structured and sourced, but you should confirm specifics with the cited
      authority until the label is removed.
    </p>

    <h2>This is not legal advice</h2>
    <p>
      The site is informational. Procedures change and individual circumstances vary;
      always confirm with the relevant Italian authority before acting.
    </p>

    <p>
      Spotted something out of date?
      <a href="/about#contribute">Tell us</a> — reader reports feed directly into our
      review queue.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
